>
  <p>
    Email is one of the oldest parts of the internet. When the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) was standardized in the early 1980s, the network was mostly used by universities and researchers who trusted each other. Spam, tracking pixels and data brokers didn't exist yet.
  </p>

  <p>
    As the web went mainstream in the late 1990s and early 2000s, email addresses became targets for marketers and spammers. One response to the flood of junk mail was the <strong>disposable temporary email service</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Early Days: The Usenet Spam Crisis & The Birth of Mailinator</h2>
  
  <p>
    In the late 1990s, spammers collected addresses from Usenet groups and web message boards. People fought back with "email munging" (writing <em>user[at]domain[dot]com</em>) or by creating spare Hotmail or Yahoo accounts, but every new webmail account meant another signup form.
  </p>

  <p>
    In 2003, developer Paul Tyma launched <strong>Mailinator</strong>, widely seen as the first popular disposable email service. Any email sent to <em>anything@mailinator.com</em> was accepted and could be read by anyone, with no signup or password.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    Mailinator showed that people didn't want another permanent email account. They wanted somewhere to catch a confirmation link and then forget about it.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>How Disposable Email Changed</h2>

  <p>
    Early services solved the spam problem but were completely public: anyone could type in your inbox name and read your mail. Over the next two decades, services changed in a few broad ways:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Public catch-alls:</strong> shared inboxes that anyone could read. Fast, but no privacy at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Timed inboxes:</strong> services such as 10 Minute Mail and Guerrilla Mail tied an inbox to your browser session and deleted it after a set time.</li>
    <li><strong>More domains:</strong> as websites started blocking known disposable domains, services added new ones so their users had alternatives.</li>
    <li><strong>Accounts and APIs:</strong> providers such as Mail.tm offer real inboxes with a password and a public API. Sites like <a href="/">TempoEmails</a> build on several of these providers and add conveniences such as automatic code detection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why Disposable Email Became an Essential Modern Tool</h2>

  <p>
    Disposable email isn't just a trick for dodging spam anymore. Privacy-minded users, developers and security testers all use it:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Less Tracking</h3>
  <p>
    As detailed in our analysis of <a href="/blog/why-companies-want-your-email/">why websites collect email addresses</a>, ad networks use hashed email addresses to recognize the same person across different services. A disposable address breaks that link.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Software Testing</h3>
  <p>
    Development teams use temporary addresses to test signup flows and email templates without creating real mailboxes for every test account.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Smaller Impact from Data Breaches</h3>
  <p>
    If you used a disposable address for a trial or a download, a breach at that company doesn't expose your real email. The scams and phishing that follow a breach go to an inbox you no longer use.
  </p>

  <h2>Key Milestones in the History of Temporary Email</h2>

  <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
    <div class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 space-y-1">
      <span class="text-xs font-mono text-accent-text font-semibold">1982 — RFC 821 Published</span>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">SMTP standardized by Jonathan Postel, establishing basic email architecture across the internet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 space-y-1">
      <span class="text-xs font-mono text-accent-text font-semibold">2003 — Mailinator Founded</span>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">One of the first widely known disposable email services launches.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 space-y-1">
      <span class="text-xs font-mono text-accent-text font-semibold">2018 — GDPR Takes Effect</span>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">The EU's data protection rules treat email addresses as personal data.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 space-y-1">
      <span class="text-xs font-mono text-accent-text font-semibold">2021+ — Mainstream Adoption of "Hide My Email"</span>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">Apple adds email masking to iCloud+, and similar features follow elsewhere, bringing throwaway addresses to a mainstream audience.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Future of Disposable Email and Digital Privacy</h2>

  <p>
    Some directions disposable email may take in the next few years:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Better code and link extraction:</strong> inboxes that pull out the code or link you need and hide the rest of the email.</li>
    <li><strong>End-to-end encrypted inboxes:</strong> temporary mailboxes whose contents even the provider can't read.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose-your-own lifetime:</strong> inboxes where you set how long they last, from a minute to several days.</li>
  </ul>

